Convention check: every external link on the landing page uses `target="_blank" rel="noopener"` (App Store badges, TestFlight, Windows download). The macOS button previously had it; this PR silently REMOVES it, making the macOS button the only external download button that navigates the current tab away. The extension-download button (line 326) also lacks target="_blank" — a direct zip download link (same-tab download is standard for direct downloads; browser stays on page because Content-Disposition: attachment).

Nuance: for a DIRECT FILE download (Content-Disposition attachment), same-tab is fine and actually preferred — the browser stays on the page. When the resolver succeeds, the macOS button hrefs a DMG URL → attachment download → user stays on the landing page. Good UX, arguably better than before.

BUT in the FALLBACK path (resolver failed/rate-limited), the button href stays `releases/latest` — an HTML PAGE. Clicking it now navigates the current tab to GitHub, taking the user OFF the fa1.dev landing page. Previously it opened in a new tab (staying on the landing). Same-tab navigation to an HTML page is the actual regression scenario. Same for the app-less subtitle path — wait, that's the "builds: see releases" link inside the subtitle (no target either).

How severe? It's a UX change, unmentioned in the PR description which claims the button "keps its static releases-page href" (true) but doesn't mention dropping target/rel. The Windows button right below keeps target="_blank" pointing at the SAME releases page — inconsistent: mac fallback → same-tab navigate away; windows → new tab. This is a real, small behavioral inconsistency on a touched line. Severity: SUGESTION (clarity/behavior polish — not a bug that breaks function; the link works). Per the necessity test: does it defend correctness on a touched path? It's a UX/consistency issue on a changed line — borderline. I'd classify 💡 SUGESTION. Not blocking.

Next: check the E2E fixtures leg for a bug — `withApi` uses `ctx.route(...)` with a regex requiring `releases\?` — fine. And the browser-level abort `ctx.route('https://api.github.com/**', abort)` registered at browser context creation, then per-context routes registered later in `withApi` win. Correct per Playwright docs (later routes take precedence).

One concern: `waitResolved` timeout 500 — fine.

Another concern in the E2E: the draft fixture `rel('v1.0.735', [...], true)` — draft: true. But resolver's draft check `releases[i].draft` — matches.

Now, the `direct_download_test.dart` static pin "landing page leaks no unguaranteed latest/download deep link (AC6)": regex `https?://[^"' <>()+,\t]*/releases/latest/download/...` — on the landing page. That's fine.

Now — a subtle CHECK on dl.js's `named` logic:

```js
for (var j = 0; j < assets.length; j++) {
 var a = assets[j];
 if (dmgRe('arm64').test(a.name) && !url.arm64) {
   url.arm64 = a.browser_download_url;
   named = named || a.name;
 }
 if (dmgRe('x86_64').test(a.name) && !url.x86_64) {
   url.x86_64 = a.browser_download_url;
   named = named || a.name;
 }
``

`named` takes the FIRST matched asset's name — could be either arch; the version is identical across arches (same release version), so versionOf(named) is fine. If the first match is the stable name (no version) → version ''. When would the stable name match first? Asset ordering on GitHub releases is alphabetical-ish by name: 'fa-nosandbox-macos-arm64.dmg' sorts BEFORE 'fa-macos-nosandbox-arm64-1.0.740.dmg'? GitHub API returns assets sorted by... actually assets are returned in upload order (or sorted by name? The GitHub API returns assets sorted by name/id). GitHub list assets are returned sorted by asset ID (upload order) — release_attach_or_create uploads ASETS in order: $DMGS first (versioned names), then stable copies appended at the end (STABLE_COPIES appended AFTER DMGS in ASETS). So versioned twins upload FIRST → named = versioned name → version extracted correctly. In the STABLE_ONLY fixture (only stable names) version = '' → subtitle shows " · arm64" with leading empty version. Cosmetic edge, only in the transitional scenario where a release carries only stable names (won't happen in the real pipeline since versioned DMGs are always attached alongside). Fine — not worth a thread. Maybe digest-worthy NIT? I'll put it in digest.

Another look — `sub.append(res.version + ' · ' + arch)` when version='' gives " · arm64". Ugly but only in the stable-only scenario. Digest.

Now — CACHE CROSS-SURFACE: landing caches to localStorage on fa1.dev (same origin as /dl/macos/), shared. Good.

Cache write happens BEFORE user actually confirms the URL works — fine.

XSS: `a.href = res.url[alt]` — res.url comes from GitHub API JSON. If compromised (MITM impossible over https), javascript: URL risk is theoretical; GitHub API is trusted. Standard practice. Fine.

One more: `archHint()` — creates a canvas + WebGL context on EVERY page load (even with cached resolution — wire() calls archHint() before readCache). Minor perf, fine. But headless Chromium (E2E goldens) — archHint returns arm64; deterministic. OK.

An important check — dl.js `wire()` on the landing page when `sub` is hidden and resolution succeeds → subtitle unhidden → the REG goldens pin the API-aborted render (subtitle hidden). The claim: screenshots byte-identical to main. Plausible.

But WAIT — one significant thing to verify: the REG golden leg in site_mobile_check.mjs — does it run against a LOCAL server where the page loads dl.js?v=1? The abort route kills api.github.com. dl.js fails → catch → fallback. Subtitle stays hidden. Golden-identical. OK.

Also — mobile-check AC1/AC4 scroll guard runs on ALL pages including the NEW page /dl/macos/? The scroll guard loops over pages — let me check which pages list. If /dl/macos/ is not in the pages list, fine either way.

Another check — `test/site/` dart tests: let me actually RUN the site tests to confirm they pass (the PR claims 83/83). Also check `dart analyze` on the new test file maybe. Let me run the site tests.

Also verify: are there other references to `releases/latest` for macOS in README/site that should have been updated (consistency)? E.g. docs, install scripts. The PR only changed README's macOS-app link. Let me grep for remaining stale references to the macos DMG links elsewhere in docs/site — e.g. site README or landing FAQ sections mentioning "releases/latest" for macOS.

